    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Recent dynamic displacement studies have indicated that the irreducible water saturation may increase on temperature level increase under drainage. The speculation was made that capillary pressure saturation data should also change to show this effect. Although there have been capillary pressure saturation studies which show important differences between laboratory and reservoir conditions (presumably including a temperature level change), the effects have usually been attributed to adsorption and desorption of polar-nonpolar components from the liquid phases. There appears to be no systematic study of the effect of temperature level upon capillary pressure.In the present work, an instrument was constructed to permit measurement of capillary pressures of porous media at elevated temperatures. Drainage and imbibition cycles for positive capillary pressures were measured for two consolidated sandstones and one limestone sample, for either three or four isotherms ranging from 70?F to 325?F. The fluids used were distilled water and white mineral oilResults for the sandstone samples were similar. The irreducible water saturation increased significantly as temperatures was increased. This was accompanied by a decrease in the apparent residual oil saturation. Also, the hysteresis between drainage and imbibition cycles decreased as temperature level increased, and was nearly absent at 300?F. The results indicated that the sandstone samples became markedly more water-wet as temperature level increased. The results for the limestone sample were quite different. Most capillary pressure-saturation curves for the various isotherms were found to lie within the envelope of the room temperature drainage and imbibition curves with no significant change with temperature. Interpretation of the results based upon thermodynamic considerations was made. The data were used to calculate relative permeabilities and relative permeability ratios and the results were compared with those of Poston, et. al., and Davidson.",
